<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shafi'i school</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shafi'i</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shafi'i school is one of the four major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamic law, named after the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shafii">al-Shafi'i</a>. It became widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> institutionalised Shafi'i jurisprudence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> followed Shafi'i and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law in its legal administration.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shafi'i</b> jurisprudence predominated at the Ayyubid court, though the dynasty also endowed institutions for the other Sunni schools.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam of the <b>Shafi'i school</b> became central to court and urban life, while Brunei's brass-casters became famous across the region for their cannon and vessels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many highlanders of Ghor had followed the Karramiyya, a pietist Sunni movement, but Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad later adopted the <b>Shafi'i school</b> while Mu'izz al-Din became a Hanafi, and the theologian Fakhr al-Din al-Razi was active at Ghurid courts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religiously the country divides between Zaydi Shia Muslims in the northern highlands and <b>Shafi'i</b> Sunni Muslims in the south, along the coastal plain and in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nizamiyya madrasas in Baghdad, Nishapur, Isfahan and elsewhere promoted <b>Shafi'i</b> jurisprudence and Ash'ari theology, and helped to standardise Sunni learning and legal practice across the empire's diverse territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…such as Ibn Hajar al-Haytami, Nazifi and Qastallani wrote commentaries on it, over 90 in all, and the <b>Shafi'i</b> hadith master Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani (d. 852 AH) studied it, reading it aloud to his teacher and receiving it in writing from a transmitter who had heard it from…</blockquote><a href="/songs/al-burda">Al-Burda</a></li></ul></section>
